%[text] # WD Servo Analysis with Signal Processing and Control Apps
%[text] This app-first workshop follows a realistic servo engineering workflow using MATLAB, Signal Processing Toolbox, and Control System Toolbox. It does not require Simulink or Simscape. The included signals and plant model are illustrative and must be replaced with validated WD data and parameters before engineering decisions are made.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Workshop flow"}
%[text] - Import drive and FA measurement data
%[text] - Explore signals with Signal Analyzer
%[text] - Investigate filters with Filter Designer
%[text] - Compare dynamic responses with Linear System Analyzer
%[text] - Tune a controller with a Live Task and PID Tuner
%[text] - Shape the loop interactively with Control System Designer \
%%
%[text] ## 1. Available apps for the WD Servo team
%[text:table]
%[text] | Product | App or interactive tool | Customer value |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Signal Processing Toolbox | Signal Analyzer | Import, preprocess, compare, measure, and inspect spectra |
%[text] | Signal Processing Toolbox | Filter Designer | Explore FIR and IIR specifications, responses, and generated code |
%[text] | Signal Processing Toolbox | Signal Labeler | Mark events or regions for review and future data workflows |
%[text] | Control System Toolbox | Linear System Analyzer | Compare step, Bode, Nyquist, Nichols, and pole-zero responses |
%[text] | Control System Toolbox | PID Tuner | Balance response speed, robustness, tracking, and disturbance rejection |
%[text] | Control System Toolbox | Control System Designer | Perform interactive Bode, root-locus, and Nichols loop shaping |
%[text] | Control System Toolbox | Tune PID Controller Live Task | Tune inside the Live Script and automatically generate MATLAB code |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## 2. Import a CSV measurement
%[text] The sample file represents position error, motor current, and acceleration from one servo axis. Local engineers can replace it with a CSV exported from an FA or drive-communication workflow.
workshopFolder = fileparts(which('WD_Servo_Signal_Control_Workshop'));
data = readtable(fullfile(workshopFolder, 'sample_servo_data.csv'));
sampleRate_Hz = 1/median(diff(data.Time_s))
servoSignals = timetable(seconds(data.Time_s), data.PositionError_um, data.MotorCurrent_A, data.Acceleration_g, ...
    VariableNames={'PositionError_um','MotorCurrent_A','Acceleration_g'});
signalSummary = table(string(servoSignals.Properties.VariableNames)', ...
    [rms(servoSignals.PositionError_um); rms(servoSignals.MotorCurrent_A); rms(servoSignals.Acceleration_g)], ...
    VariableNames={'Signal','RMS'})
%%
%[text] ## 3. Signal Processing App — Signal Analyzer
%[text] ![](text:image:4dbb)
%[text] Set `openSignalAnalyzer` to `true` and run this section, or open **Apps \> Signal Analyzer**. The timetable transfers all three synchronized channels and their time information into the App.
%[text] Demonstrate this workflow in Signal Analyzer:
%[text] 1. Plot position error, motor current, and acceleration in separate displays.
%[text] 2. Add a **Spectrum** view and identify the vibration component near 120 Hz.
%[text] 3. Zoom around a transient and use panners or data cursors.
%[text] 4. Duplicate acceleration before preprocessing so the original remains available.
%[text] 5. Try Detrend, Smooth, Lowpass, or Bandstop and inspect the preprocessing history.
%[text] 6. Open **Measurements** for RMS, peaks, and signal statistics.
%[text] 7. Select **Generate Function** to convert the accepted App workflow into reusable MATLAB code. \
openSignalAnalyzer = false;
if openSignalAnalyzer
    signalAnalyzer(servoSignals)
end
%%
%[text] ## 4. Code checkpoint generated from signal analysis
%[text] Signal Analyzer is the main demonstration. This compact code checkpoint confirms the frequency that the customer finds interactively and shows how App-generated code can be reused in an existing US or Japan script.
[powerSpectrum, frequency_Hz] = pwelch(data.Acceleration_g, hann(1024), 512, 2048, sampleRate_Hz);
[~, dominantIndex] = max(powerSpectrum);
dominantFrequency_Hz = frequency_Hz(dominantIndex)
ax = axes;
plot(ax, frequency_Hz, pow2db(powerSpectrum), LineWidth=1.2)
xlim(ax, [0 500])
grid(ax, 'on')
xlabel(ax, 'Frequency (Hz)')
ylabel(ax, 'PSD (dB/Hz)')
xline(ax, dominantFrequency_Hz, '--r', compose('%.1f Hz',dominantFrequency_Hz))
title(ax, 'Spectrum confirmed after interactive exploration')
%%
%[text] ## 5. Signal Processing App — Filter Designer
%[text] Open **Apps \> Filter Designer** or set `openFilterDesigner` to `true`. Begin with the measured sample rate of `2000 Hz`. Use the spectrum and the engineering objective to discuss passband, stopband, phase, latency, and implementation constraints before selecting a design.
%[text] Suggested talking points:
%[text] - Compare FIR and IIR magnitude and phase responses.
%[text] - Explain why a display that looks smoother is not enough to approve a filter.
%[text] - Show how to export a digital filter object or generate MATLAB code.
%[text] - Use the generated code inside an existing analysis script or future application. \
openFilterDesigner = false;
if openFilterDesigner
    filterDesigner
end
%%
%[text] ## 6. Create an illustrative servo plant model
%[text] Control System Toolbox works with transfer-function, state-space, zero-pole-gain, and frequency-response models. Here `servoPlant` is an illustrative SISO plant used to demonstrate the apps; it is not a model of a WD production drive.
servoPlant = tf(1, [0.018 0.22 1]);
startingController = pid(4, 12, 0.02);
startingClosedLoop = feedback(startingController*servoPlant, 1);
plantInformation = table(pole(servoPlant), VariableNames={'PlantPoles'})
%%
%[text] ## 7. Control App — Linear System Analyzer
%[text] ![](text:image:33bf)
%[text] Set `openLinearSystemAnalyzer` to `true`. The App opens with step and Bode plots so the customer can move between time-domain performance and frequency-domain robustness without writing plotting code.
%[text] In the App, right-click the plots to show rise time, settling time, overshoot, stability margins, and other characteristics. Later, import additional candidate models to compare them in the same session.
openLinearSystemAnalyzer = false;
if openLinearSystemAnalyzer
    linearSystemAnalyzer(["step","bode"], servoPlant, startingClosedLoop)
end
%%
%[text] ## 8. Live Task — Tune PID Controller
%[text] ![](text:image:991d)
%[text] Place the cursor below this paragraph and select **Live Editor \> Task \> Tune PID Controller**. Choose `servoPlant`, select a PID controller with derivative filter, and name the output `tunedController`. Compare time-domain and frequency-domain goals, then adjust response time or bandwidth and phase margin while watching the response plot.
%[text] Change **Design focus** between Balanced, Reference tracking, and Input disturbance rejection. Reveal the generated code to show how the result can be reviewed and shared with the US or Japan development team.
%[text] The following lines are the generated-code fallback so the workshop can continue even if the Live Task is not inserted.
targetBandwidth_rad_s = 2*pi*8;
[tunedController, tuningInformation] = pidtune(servoPlant, 'PIDF', targetBandwidth_rad_s);
tunedClosedLoop = feedback(tunedController*servoPlant, 1);
tunedController
%%
%[text] ## 9. Control App — PID Tuner
%[text] ![](text:image:141f)
%[text] Set `openPIDTuner` to `true` to launch PID Tuner with the illustrative plant and the starting controller as a baseline. This standalone App is useful when the main goal is rapid PID tuning rather than documenting every step in a Live Script.
%[text] Demonstrate the response-time and transient-behavior controls, add Reference Tracking and Input Disturbance Rejection plots, enable the baseline comparison, and export the selected controller to the MATLAB workspace.
openPIDTuner = false;
if openPIDTuner
    pidTuner(servoPlant, startingController)
end
%%
%[text] ## 10. Control App — Control System Designer
%[text] Set `openControlSystemDesigner` to `true` to initialize Control System Designer with Bode and root-locus editors. This is the visual workflow for engineers who want to shape the loop by adding or moving controller poles, zeros, and gain, then verify closed-loop responses.
%[text] Show stability margins, drag the design graphically, store a candidate design, compare responses, and export the selected compensator. This MATLAB plant workflow requires Control System Toolbox; Simulink-based plant linearization would require additional products and is outside this workshop.
openControlSystemDesigner = false;
if openControlSystemDesigner
    controlSystemDesigner({'bode','rlocus'}, servoPlant, startingController)
end
%%
%[text] ## 11. Quantify the controller comparison
%[text] The apps support interactive decisions; MATLAB code makes the accepted design repeatable. This section compares the starting and tuned controllers and checks reference tracking and load-disturbance rejection.
startingMetrics = stepinfo(startingClosedLoop);
tunedMetrics = stepinfo(tunedClosedLoop);
controlComparison = table( ...
    [startingMetrics.RiseTime;tunedMetrics.RiseTime], ...
    [startingMetrics.SettlingTime;tunedMetrics.SettlingTime], ...
    [startingMetrics.Overshoot;tunedMetrics.Overshoot], ...
    RowNames={'Starting controller','Tuned controller'}, ...
    VariableNames={'RiseTime_s','SettlingTime_s','Overshoot_pct'})
responseTime_s = (0:0.0005:1.2)';
startingResponse = step(startingClosedLoop, responseTime_s);
tunedResponse = step(tunedClosedLoop, responseTime_s);
disturbancePath = feedback(servoPlant, tunedController);
loadDisturbance = 0.18*double(responseTime_s >= 0.30 & responseTime_s < 0.45);
positionDeviation = lsim(disturbancePath, loadDisturbance, responseTime_s);
tiledlayout(2,1)
nexttile
plot(responseTime_s, startingResponse, LineWidth=1.1)
hold on
plot(responseTime_s, tunedResponse, LineWidth=1.4)
yline(1, '--')
hold off
grid on
ylabel('Position')
legend('Starting','Tuned','Command', Location='southeast')
title('Reference tracking')
nexttile
plot(responseTime_s, loadDisturbance, '--', LineWidth=1.1)
hold on
plot(responseTime_s, positionDeviation, LineWidth=1.4)
hold off
grid on
xlabel('Time (s)')
ylabel('Normalized value')
legend('Load disturbance','Position deviation')
title('Input-disturbance rejection')
%%
%[text] ## 12. Recommended live demonstration order
%[text] 1. Use Signal Analyzer first because it starts from the customer measurement.
%[text] 2. Use Filter Designer only after identifying the signal content and filter objective.
%[text] 3. Create or import a validated plant model.
%[text] 4. Use Linear System Analyzer to understand the uncontrolled and baseline responses.
%[text] 5. Tune with the Tune PID Controller Live Task or PID Tuner.
%[text] 6. Use Control System Designer when graphical loop shaping is valuable.
%[text] 7. Export the accepted result and integrate the generated code into WD scripts. \
%%
%[text] ## 13. Discussion with the Servo team
%[text] - Which US or Japan scripts are customized most often in Thailand?
%[text] - Which measured channels and frequencies indicate good or bad drive behavior?
%[text] - Is the analysis offline, or must the final filter/controller run in real time?
%[text] - Which response limits define success: RMS, resonance amplitude, bandwidth, phase margin, overshoot, settling time, or disturbance rejection?
%[text] - Which App-generated workflow should become a standardized Live Script or a future App Designer application? \

